Agent: That’s a meaningful project — learning a refugee’s story can be powerful. Below I’ll list practical sources and steps you can use to research your great-aunt’s experience (U.S. and German avenues), plus tips for searching and next steps I can help with.

Key repositories & resources
- USCIS Genealogy Program (U.S. records)
  - Request immigrant/alien files, naturalization papers, visa files. Useful if she later immigrated to the U.S. (Form G-1041 / G-639 process).
  - https://www.uscis.gov/genealogy

- National Archives (NARA, U.S.)
  - Passenger arrival lists, naturalization records, WWII/DP-era refugee records, State Dept. files.
  - Search ARC catalog and NARA databases; request copies when relevant.
  - https://www.archives.gov

- International Tracing Service / Arolsen Archives (Bad Arolsen, Germany)
  - Holds records of displaced persons (DPs), deportations, camp registrations and tracing files from WWII and immediate postwar years.
  - Online search and request procedures; they assist families tracing displaced relatives.
  - https://arolsen-archives.org

- U.S. Holocaust Memorial Museum (USHMM)
  - Collections, oral histories, Holocaust-era refugee documentation, refugee aid organization records, DP camp info.
  - https://www.ushmm.org

- Yad Vashem / other Holocaust archives
  - If there is any Holocaust-related context, Yad Vashem and European archives may hold testimonies, lists, or municipal records.

- Bundesarchiv (German Federal Archives) & regional archives (Landesarchive)
  - Civil/register records, refugee and expellee records, local wartime administration documents. For pre- and post-war German records you may need to contact the appropriate state archive for the town where she was born or lived.

- International Committee of the Red Cross (ICRC) tracing
  - ICRC tracing service can assist in certain wartime separation cases and hold some records.

- Local church/parish records (Germany)
  - Baptism, confirmation, or residency records. Often digitized or available through local registry offices (Standesamt).

- JewishGen, Arolsen, and other community-specific databases
  - If applicable, community genealogical resources can be invaluable.

- Commercial genealogy services / databases
  - Ancestry.com, FamilySearch.org (free), MyHeritage. These include passenger lists, census data, naturalization records, and often user-submitted family trees or uploaded documents.

- Newspapers & published oral histories
  - Contemporary newspapers, refugee organization newsletters, or local histories sometimes recount refugee resettlement and could mention names or groups.

Practical search steps and tips
1. Gather everything you already know
   - Full name (including maiden and alternate spellings), date/place of birth, last known residence in Germany, date or approximate year she left Germany, ports of departure/arrival, names of sponsors/relatives in U.S., naturalization date if applicable, any documents (photos, letters, travel papers, ship name).

2. Start with family sources
   - Ask relatives for documents (passports, naturalization certificates, letters), oral stories, photographs, or people who might recall details.

3. Check U.S. entry records
   - Search passenger lists (Ancestry, FamilySearch, NARA) for arrivals by name and year. Try variants and possible Anglicizations.

4. Search naturalization & visa files
   - If she naturalized, naturalization petitions often include place of birth, arrival port, and sponsors. USCIS Genealogy and NARA hold many of these files.

5. Look into DP/Refugee records
   - If she came through displaced persons camps or UNRRA, the Arolsen Archives and UN/Red Cross records are essential.

6. German records / local archives
   - If you know town of origin, contact municipal archives (Standesamt) or the state archive for birth, marriage, and residence records.

7. Use name/variant strategies
   - Try multiple spellings, diminutives, and translations. Include mother/father names if known — that helps group records.

8. Translate and verify
   - Many documents will be in German; use professional translations for official records. Verify each record with multiple sources.

9. Consider professional help
   - If you hit brick walls, a professional genealogist experienced in German/WWII-era research can save time and access restricted archives.

What to watch for / privacy & legal notes
- Some German records have privacy restrictions (e.g., civil registry access rules). Archives can advise on requirements.
- Arolsen/ITS and other institutions may require proof of relationship for certain requests.
- Expect fees and processing times, especially for file retrievals and translations.

How I can help next
- Draft request letters/email templates for archives (e.g., USCIS genealogy request, Arolsen inquiry, local Standesamt).
- Suggest exact search queries and name variants for passenger lists and FamilySearch/Ancestry.
- Help you organize the information you already have into a timeline and checklist of records to request.

If you share what specifics you already have about your great-aunt (full name, birth year/place, year she left Germany or arrived in the U.S., any known ports, any documents you possess), I can propose targeted searches and draft the first request for an archive or online database.
